    <p><term>Instructions:</term> The exercises here should be completed in groups
of 2 or 3 students with one write-up submitted from each group.
You may write directly on this handout.  When asked for an
explanation, please use complete sentences.  Be sure to include
everyone's name on the write-up.</p>
    <p>Up to this point, we've have looked at differential 
equations that describe a single function.  It sometimes happens,
however, that we find two quantities whose rates of change depend on
one another.  In this case, we have a system of differential equations.</p>
    <p>In today's lab, we'll begin by looking
at a system of differential equations that can be used to describe
the interaction of two different species, one of whom preys on the
other.  For instance, we will consider a population of wolves <m>W(t)</m>
that prey upon a population of deer <m>D(t)</m>.  Let's build two
differential equations that describe how the populations change.</p>
    <p>First of all, we will suppose that if there were no wolves, the deer
would increase at a rate proportional to their population:
<md>
\frac{dD}{dt} = 0.08D \quad\mbox{or}\quad\frac{dD/dt}{D} = 0.08
</md>
to be precise where time <m>t</m> is measured in days.  Notice this means that the per capita growth rate is constant.</p>
    <p>If there are now some wolves
around, we would expect that the per capita growth rate would decrease.  For instance, we might find that 
<md>
\frac{dD/dt}{D} = (0.08 - 0.001W)\quad\mbox{or}\quad
\frac{dD}{dt} = (0.08 - 0.001W)D.
</md></p>
    <p>In the same way, we will suppose that if there were no deer, the
wolves would die off, since they have no food, at a rate proportional
to their population:
<md>
\frac{dW}{dt} = -0.02W.
</md>
However, if there are some deer around, we expect the
wolves' per capita growth rate to increase.  For instance, 
<md>
\frac{dW/dt}{W} = (-0.02 + 0.00002D)\quad\mbox{or}\quad
\frac{dW}{dt} = (-0.02 + 0.00002D)W.
</md></p>
    <p>The pair of equations
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dD}{dt} \amp = \amp (0.08 - 0.001W) D</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dW}{dt} \amp = \amp (-0.02 + 0.00002D)W</mrow>
</md>
is called the Lotka-Volterra model for predator-prey interaction.
Though a simple model, the basic effects predicted by the model have
been observed in nature.</p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose at some time, there are 1500 deer and 60 wolves.
  Is the deer population increasing or decreasing?  Is the wolf
  population increasing or decreasing?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>For what values of <m>W</m> does the deer population increase?
  For what values of <m>D</m> does the wolf population increase?  Does this
  agree with your intuition about the situation?  Why or why not?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>Are there values for <m>D</m> and <m>W</m> so that neither quantity
  changes?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose initially there are <m>1000</m> deer and <m>40</m> wolves;
  that is, <m>D(0) = 1000</m> and <m>W(0) = 40</m>.  Use Excel to implement 
  Euler's method with a step size of <m>h=0.1</m> to find out how the
  populations behave over the 
  course of one year.</p>
        <p>Your spreadsheet will probably begin something like this:</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>h</m> &amp; <m>t_k</m> &amp; <m>D_k</m> &amp; <m>W_k</m> &amp; <m>\Delta D</m> &amp; <m>\Delta W</m> 
      \hline
      \hline</p>
        <p>0.1 &amp; 0.0 &amp; 1000.0 &amp; 40.0 &amp;4.00 &amp;0.00 
      0.1 &amp; 0.1 &amp; 1004.0 &amp; 40.0 &amp;4.02 &amp;0.00 
      0.1 &amp; 0.2 &amp; 1008.0 &amp; 40.0 &amp;4.03 &amp;0.00 
      0.1 &amp; 0.3 &amp; 1012.0 &amp; 40.0 &amp;4.05 &amp;0.00 
      %1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1000.00 &amp; 40.00 &amp; 40.00 &amp; 0.00
      %1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1040.00 &amp; 40.00 &amp; 41.60 &amp; 0.03
      %1 &amp; 2 &amp; 1081.60 &amp; 40.03 &amp; 43.23 &amp; 0.07
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
        <p>Sketch a graph of the populations <m>D(t)</m> and <m>W(t)</m> over one
  year below and discuss any interesting
  features that you see.  In particular, you should discuss the
  general behavior of the two graphs along with any relationships you
  notice between the two graphs.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>We'll now examine another system of differential equations
  but now we will imagine we have two species <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that compete
  for food.  If there is one species without the other, we will assume
  a logistic model for population growth:  say,
  <md>
  \frac{dx}{dt}=x(1-x)
  </md>
  where <m>x</m> is measured in millions.  However, if there is some of the
  species <m>y</m> around, it will lower the growth rate of <m>x</m> to, say,
  <md>
  \frac{dx}{dt}=x(1-x-y).
  </md>
  Species <m>y</m> behaves in the same way:
  <md>
  \frac{dy}{dt}=y(0.75-y-0.5x)
  </md>
  so we have the system of equations
  <md>
<mrow>\frac{dx}{dt}\amp=\ampx(1-x-y)</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dy}{dt}\amp=\ampy(0.75-y-0.5x)</mrow>
</md></p>
        <p>Set up a spreadsheet to study this system of equations with the
    intial values <m>x(0) = 1.5</m> and <m>y(0)=0.25</m> using a step size of
    <m>h=0.1</m>.  Make a plot of <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>.  What happens after a
    very long time?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Study what happens with a few different values of the initial
    values.  What do you notice happens here?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If the constants are adjusted just a little bit, there is a very
    different kind of behavior.  Consider the system of equations
  <md>
<mrow>\frac{dx}{dt}\amp=\ampx(1-x-y)</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dy}{dt}\amp=\ampy(0.5-0.25y-0.75x)</mrow>
</md></p>
        <p>Study this system with <m>x(0) = 1.5</m> and <m>y(0)=0.25</m>.  Again, make
    a plot of <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>.  What happens after a very long time?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now consider the initial values <m>x(0) = 0.5</m> and <m>y(0)=1</m>.  What
    happens after a very long time?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How do these two examples of systems of competing species differ
    from one another?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
